"The Hilton-Milner theorem in the $\\ell_2$-norm (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12433)\nciteturn3academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Title: The Hilton-Milner theorem in the $\\ell_2$-norm\nTitle: The Hilton-Milner theorem in the $\\ell_2$-norm\nAuthors: George Brooks, William Linz\nDate: Thu Oct  8 17:57:14 2026\n\nThe Hilton-Milner theorem is a classical theorem in extremal set theory which determines the maximum size of a nontrivial $k$-uniform intersecting family. In this paper, we prove an exact version of the Hilton-Milner theorem in the $\\ell_2$-norm. Our method also gives an asymptotic version of the Hilton-Milner theorem in the $(j, p)$-norm.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistributions of statistics on separable permutations (https://zhangbiaomath.github.io/papers/2024-dam-separable-perms.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Distributions of statistics on separable permutations ... Finally, we present three unimodality conjectures about distributions ... Keywords: separable permutation, irreducible permutation, permutation\nstatistic, distribution\nDistributions of statistics on separable permutations\nJoanna N. Chena, Sergey Kitaevb and Philip B. Zhang*c\naCollege of Science, Tianjin University of Technology\nTianjin 300384, P. R. China\nbDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Strathclyde\n26 Richmond Street, Glasgow G1 1XH, United Kingdom\ncCollege of Mathematical Science, Tianjin Normal University\nTianjin 300387, P. R. China\nEmail: ajoannachen@tjut.edu.cn, bsergey.kitaev@cis.strath.ac.uk,\nczhang@tjnu.edu.cn\nAbstract. We derive functional equations for distributions of six classi-\ncal statistics (ascents, descents, left-to-right maxima, right-to-left maxima,\nleft-to-right minima, and right-to-left minima) on separable and irreducible\nseparable permutations. The equations are used to find a third degree equa-\ntion for joint distribution of ascents and descents on separable permuta-\ntions that generalizes the respective known result for the descent distribu-\ntion. Moreover, our general functional equations allow us to derive explicitly\n(joint) distribution of any subset of maxima and minima statistics on irre-\nducible, reducible and all separable permutations. In particular, there are\ntwo equivalence classes of distributions of a pair of maxima or minima statis-\ntics. Finally, we present three unimodality conjectures about distributions\nof statistics on separable permutations.\nAMS Classification 2020: 05A15\nKeywords: separable permutation, irreducible permutation, permutation\nstatistic, distribution\n1\nIntroduction\nA permutation of length n is a rearrangement of the set [n] := {1, 2, . . . , n}.\nDenote by Sn the set of permutations of [n] and let ε be the empty permu-\ntation. A pattern is a permutation. A permutation π1π2 · · · πn ∈Sn avoids\na pattern p = p1p2 · · · pk ∈Sk if there is no subsequence πi1πi2 · · · πik such\n*Corresponding author\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistributions of statistics on separable permutations (https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.18517)\nciteturn3academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Moreover, our general functional equations allow us to derive explicitly (joint) distribution of any subset of maxima and minima statistics on irreducible, reducible and all separable permutations. ... Finally, we present three unimodality conjectures about distributions of statistics on separable permutations.\nTitle: Distributions of statistics on separable permutations\nAuthors: Joanna N. Chen, Sergey Kitaev, Philip B. Zhang\nDate: Mon Apr 29 09:02:09 2024\n\nWe derive functional equations for distributions of six classical statistics (ascents, descents, left-to-right maxima, right-to-left maxima, left-to-right minima, and right-to-left minima) on separable and irreducible separable permutations. The equations are used to find a third degree equation for joint distribution of ascents and descents on separable permutations that generalizes the respective known result for the descent distribution. Moreover, our general functional equations allow us to derive explicitly (joint) distribution of any subset of maxima and minima statistics on irreducible, reducible and all separable permutations. In particular, there are two equivalence classes of distributions of a pair of maxima or minima statistics. Finally, we present three unimodality conjectures about distributions of statistics on separable permutations.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistributions of statistics on separable permutations - Strathprints (https://strathprints.strath.ac.uk/89387/)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; Moreover, our general functional equations allow us to derive explicitly (joint) distribution of any subset of maxima and minima statistics on irreducible, reducible and all separable permutations. ... Finally, we present three unimodality conjectures about distributions of statistics on separable permutations.\n\n# Distributions of statistics on separable permutations\n\nChen, Joanna N. and Kitaev, Sergey and Zhang, Philip B. (2024) Distributions of statistics on separable permutations. Discrete Applied Mathematics, 355. pp. 169-179. ISSN 0166-218X (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2024.05.004)\n\nImagePreview  | Text. Filename: Distributions-of-statistics-on-separable-permutations.pdf\nPreprint\nLicense:\nDownload (206kB)| Preview\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe derive functional equations for distributions of six classi- cal statistics (ascents, descents, left-to-right maxima, right-to-left maxima, left-to-right minima, and right-to-left minima) on separable and irreducible separable permutations. The equations are used to find a third degree equation for joint distribution of ascents and descents on separable permuta- tions that generalizes the respective known result for the descent distribution. Moreover, our general functional equations allow us to derive explicitly (joint) distribution of any subset of maxima and minima statistics on irreducible, reducible and all separable permutations. In particular, there are two equivalence classes of distributions of a pair of maxima or minima statistics. Finally, we present three unimodality conjectures about distributions of statistics on separable permutations.\n\n#### ORCID iDs\n\nChen, Joanna N., Kitaev, Sergey ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3324-1647 and Zhang, Philip B.;\n\n  * [Input] Item metadata\n\nItem type:  | Article\nID code:  | 89387\nDates:  | Date Event 15 October 2024 Published 15 May 2024 Published Online 7 May 2024 Accepted\nSubjects:  | Science > Mathematics > Probabilities. Mathematical statistics\nDepartment:  | Faculty of Science > Mathematics and Statistics\nDepositing user:  | Pure Administrator\nDate deposited:  | 28 May 2024 14:25\nLast modified:  | 28 Aug 2026 07:58\nRelated URLs:  | \n    * https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.18517\nURI:  | https://strathprints.strath.ac.uk/id/eprint/89387--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome exact and asymptotic results for hypergraph Turán problems in ℓ2-norm - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2026.104391)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; It would be interesting to prove a version of the Hilton-Milner theorem for the ℓ2-norm.\n## Hilton-Milner [39]\n\nLet ℱ⊂([𝑛]𝑘) be a nontrivial intersecting family with 𝑛>2⁢𝑘. Then, |ℱ|≤(𝑛−1𝑘−1)−(𝑛−𝑘−1𝑘−1)+1.If 𝑘>3, equality holds only if ℱ≅ℋ⁡(𝑛,𝑘,1). If 𝑘=3, equality only holds if ℱ≅ℋ⁡(𝑛,3,1) or ℱ≅𝒜⁡(𝑛,3,1).\n\nThe 𝑡-intersecting Hilton-Milner theorem was proved by Frankl [27] for large 𝑛 and by Ahlswede and Khachatrian [3] for the optimal range 𝑛>(𝑡+1)⁢(𝑘−𝑡+1).\n\nTheorem 4.4\n\n## 𝑡-intersecting Hilton-Milner\n\nLet 𝑛,𝑘,𝑡 be integers and let ℱ⊂([𝑛]𝑘) be a nontrivial 𝑡-intersecting family. If 𝑛>(𝑡+1)⁢(𝑘−𝑡+1), then |ℱ|≤max⁡{|𝒜⁡(𝑛,𝑘,𝑡)|,|ℋ⁡(𝑛,𝑘,𝑡)|}.Furthermore, equality holds only if ℱ≅max⁡{𝒜⁡(𝑛,𝑘,𝑡),ℋ⁡(𝑛,𝑘,𝑡)}, 𝑘>2⁢𝑡+1, or ℱ≅𝒜⁡(𝑛,𝑘,𝑡), 𝑘≤2⁢𝑡+1.\n\nIt is easy to verify that for large 𝑛 |𝒜⁡(𝑛,𝑘,𝑡)|∼(𝑡+2)⁢(𝑛𝑘−𝑡−1);|ℋ⁡(𝑛,𝑘,𝑡)|∼(𝑘−𝑡+1)⁢(𝑛𝑘−𝑡−1).\n\nIt would be interesting to prove a version of the Hilton-Milner theorem for the ℓ2-norm. We first handle the case 𝑘=3.\n\nProposition 4.4\n\nLet ℱ⊂([𝑛]3) be a nontrivial intersecting family with 𝑛≥7. Then, co2⁢(ℱ)≤co2⁢(ℋ⁡(𝑛,3,1)),and equality holds if and only if ℱ≅ℋ⁡(𝑛,3,1) or ℱ≅𝒜⁡(𝑛,3,1).\n\nProof\n\nWe first compute co2⁢(ℋ⁡(𝑛,3,1)) and co2⁢(𝒜⁡(𝑛,3,1)). For ℋ⁡(𝑛,3,1), the 2-sets {1,2}, {1,3}, and {1,4} have degree 𝑛−2, the sets {1,𝑎}, 5≤𝑎≤𝑛 have degree 3, the sets {2,3},{2,4},{3,4} have degree 2, the sets {2,𝑎},{3,𝑎},{4,𝑎}, 5≤𝑎≤𝑛 each have degree 1 and the sets {𝑎,𝑏}⊂([5,𝑛]2) each have degree 0. Thus, co2⁢(ℋ⁡(𝑛,3,1))=3⁢(𝑛−2)2+32⁢(𝑛−4)+3⁢(1)2⁢(𝑛−4)+22⁢(3)=3⁢𝑛2−24.\n\nFor 𝒜⁡(𝑛,3,1), the sets {1,2},{1,3},{2,3} each have degree 𝑛−2, the sets {1,𝑎},{2,𝑎},{3,𝑎}, 4≤𝑎≤𝑛 each have degree 2, and the sets {𝑎,𝑏}⊂([4,𝑛]2) each have degree 0, so co2⁢(𝒜⁡(𝑛,3,1))=3⁢(𝑛−2)2+3⁢(2)2⁢(𝑛−3)=3⁢𝑛2−24.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContent Listing (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-mathematical-bulletin/listing?pageNum=7)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * ### A short proof of the Hilton–Milner Theorem\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### A short proof of the Hilton–Milner Theorem\n\n  * Part of:\n    * Extremal combinatorics\n  * Denys Bulavka, Russ Woodroofe\n  * Journal:\n    Canadian Mathematical Bulletin / Volume 69 / Issue 2 / June 2026\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:\n    17 October 2025, pp. 603-608\nPrint publication:\n    June 2026\n  *     * Article\n    *       * You have access Access\n      * Open access\n    * PDF\n    * HTML\n    *     * Export citation\n  * View abstract\n\nWe gi